+## D-408 — SendGrid Event Webhook receiver + suppression source (#362)
+**2026-09-21 · built, tested, SAFE TO DEPLOY EARLY · prerequisite for D-407 (#558)**
+
+**What it is.** `sendgridEventWebhook` — a public gen-1 `https.onRequest`
+endpoint that receives SendGrid Event Webhook batches, verifies their
+signature, records every event in `emailEvents`, and maintains the
+`emailSuppression` collection the Phase 2 autosender (D-407) fail-closes
+without.
+
+**Public invocation is not open access.** SendGrid must be able to reach the
+URL, so the invoker binding is `allUsers` (same pattern as `twilioWebhook`).
+The security gate is in-function: ECDSA signature verification, before any
+parsing and before any write. Unverified → `403`, nothing stored.
+
+**Scheme verified live at build time (D-079), not written from memory.**
+Source: `https://www.twilio.com/docs/sendgrid/for-developers/tracking-events/getting-started-event-webhook-security-features`
+- Signature: `X-Twilio-Email-Event-Webhook-Signature`, base64 ASN.1/DER ECDSA
+  (prime256v1). Node's default `dsaEncoding` is `der`, so `crypto.createVerify`
+  matches without transcoding.
+- Timestamp: `X-Twilio-Email-Event-Webhook-Timestamp`.
+- Digest: `sha256(timestamp || rawBody)` over the RAW request bytes. The docs
+  warn explicitly that re-serialising parsed JSON changes the bytes and breaks
+  verification — we verify `req.rawBody` and never touch `req.body`.
+- Key: the webhook's public verification key, base64 DER SubjectPublicKeyInfo,
+  stored in Secret Manager as `SENDGRID_EVENT_WEBHOOK_VERIFICATION_KEY`, same
+  discipline as `SENDGRID_API_KEY`. Never in code, never in env.
+- Replay fence: timestamps more than 10 minutes from now are refused before any
+  crypto runs.
+- Key unavailable → `503`, not "process anyway". No key means we cannot prove
+  the caller is SendGrid.
+
+**`emailEvents/{sg_event_id}`.** One doc per event, id = the base64url-safe
+`sg_event_id` (Firestore doc ids cannot contain `/`). Written with `create()`,
+so SendGrid's duplicate deliveries produce exactly ONE record and re-apply
+nothing. Stored fields: email, event, sgEventId, sgMessageId, eventAtMs,
+reason, status, type, bounceClassification, suppressing, funnel, source,
+receivedAtMs. PII discipline: no categories, no unique/custom args, no smtp-id,
+no PHI — SendGrid's own docs warn categories and unique args are not treated as
+PII on their side.
+
+**`emailSuppression/{email}` — the shared surface with D-407.** Keyed by the
+normalized email address and carrying an `email` field, because that is exactly
+what `autoInviteTick.loadSuppressedEmails()` reads. Global by address, never
+per-practice: a hard bounce is a hard bounce everywhere. `firstSuppressedAtMs`
+is set once and never moves (transactional read-then-set); the latest event
+detail wins on the other fields.
+
+**Mapping (verified against the Event Webhook Reference).** SUPPRESSION:
+`bounce` + `type:'bounce'` → `hard_bounce` (severity `hard`); `bounce` +
+`type:'blocked'` → `blocked` (severity `soft` — SendGrid calls this a temporary
+denial, so it is recorded as suppression per the #362 spec but distinguishable
+if we later want to retry soft blocks); `dropped` → `dropped`; `spamreport` →
+`spam_complaint`; `unsubscribe`/`group_unsubscribe` → `unsubscribe`.
+FUNNEL ONLY, never suppression: `processed`, `delivered`, `open`, `click`,
+`deferred`, `group_resubscribe` — these persist the invited→opened funnel past
+SendGrid's 3-day retention window (D-116).
+
+**Failure posture.** A write failure returns `500` so SendGrid retries the
+batch; the retry is safe because every store is idempotent. Malformed JSON with
+a valid signature is `400`. Invalid individual events are counted and skipped,
+never fatal to the batch.
+
+**Deploy posture — deliberately NOT gated behind cutover.** This receiver sends
+nothing and writes no patient data; it only records. Every day it is live
+before the autosender arms is a day of real bounce/complaint data in the
+suppression list, which is the whole point of #362 being D-407's prerequisite.
+
+**Operator step after deploy (not code).** In the SendGrid dashboard,
+Settings → Mail Settings → Event Webhooks: point the Post URL at this
+function's URL, enable Signed Event Webhook, save, copy the generated public
+verification key, and load it into Secret Manager as
+`SENDGRID_EVENT_WEBHOOK_VERIFICATION_KEY`. Until that secret exists the
+endpoint answers `503` and stores nothing — fail-closed by design.
+
+
